:answer-field/id #uuid "1a071a57-c0a2-5ff5-8488-5bd97002af7b" :answer-field/key "VI" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440329} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7f8d1ef-4d2a-52c3-97a7-ce498c4ce11b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439945} {:answer/id #uuid "35e5554f-349d-51bb-9ccd-94b46e947c71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7f8d1ef-4d2a-52c3-97a7-ce498c4ce11b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439945} :answer-field/id #uuid "d02f2705-c6d5-5710-8589-44dd2b88e45c" :answer-field/key "VII" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5577e429-0d55-5dd4-b588-49ac727edd6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-72440" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${x}^{2}+2xy=-5y+4$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $(-4,1)$\n- **II.** $(-4,4)$\n- **III.** $(-2,4)$\n- **IV.** $(-1,1)$\n- **V.** $(-1,4)$\n- **VI.** $(1,1)$\n- **VII.** $(4,-4)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy & =-5y+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2xy+5y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy+5y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy+5y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy+5y)j \\\\  & =(2x+5)\\,i-(2x+2y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((2x+5)\\,i-(2x+2y)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -(2x+2y) & =0 \\\\ x & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-y$ into the equation of our curve gives:    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy+5y & =4 \\\\ (-y)^{2}+2(-y)y+5y & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+5y & =4 \\\\ -{y}^{2}+5y-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-5y+4 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-4) & =0 \\\\ y & =1\\,\\text{or}\\,y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $y=1$, we get $x=-1$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,1).$\n\n- If $y=4$, we get $x=-4$ and the corresponding point is $(-4,4).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364316 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b0f044-128c-5f1b-aa69-2500dbaf5ea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-1)$ and $(0,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364318} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd3ddf3-f39a-5e23-9542-809cca8045dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(-1,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364319} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3c3702-cba3-57ad-b3e0-2c994913bd41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(0,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364320} {:answer/id #uuid "71ef2f17-55ba-507e-8bbc-fc7b6bfebfc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,0)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364321} {:answer/id #uuid "9e254c1d-c4f8-51bc-9734-35d90904ed56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(1,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdd3ddf3-f39a-5e23-9542-809cca8045dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(-1,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364319} :answer-field/id #uuid "e462412c-3cbc-5541-9147-125fc8a64b29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "32727d15-71dd-5c57-93a9-b5f15381c7c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-72416" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $2xy-3{x}^{2}=-y$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy-3{x}^{2}=-y \\\\ 2xy-3{x}^{2}+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2xy-3{x}^{2}+y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-3{x}^{2}+y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-3{x}^{2}+y)j \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\,i-2(y-3x)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((2x+1)\\,i-2(y-3x)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -2(y-3x) & =0 \\\\ y & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=3x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy-3{x}^{2}+y & =0 \\\\ 2x(3x)-3{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ 3x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=0$, we get $y=0$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=-3$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84245283-3cf9-5889-8c42-896a0c8af291" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points on a Curve at which the Tangent Vector is Perpendicular to a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices 